    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Innate immune cells and vasculature are known to interact in both development and in injury. The CNS has specific macrophages, microglia, dedicated to protecting it, but the mechanisms by which resident immune cells interact with the vasculature in the brain are not well understood. How do microglia and another key innate immune cell type, neutrophils, help or impair neurovascular development and healing? To address the roles of these immune cell types in the brain, our study used zebrafish mutants lacking microglia and macrophages to elucidate the interactions of these cells with vasculature during normal development and after blood vessel severing. Zebrafish are used as simple vertebrate model organisms because their embryos are translucent, allowing for real-time imaging of fluorescently marked cells. We characterized injury, immune response, and healing in mutants lacking microglia, and mutants lacking microglia and having uncontrolled inflammation.\r\n\r\nOur findings show that immune cells in the brain respond in similar temporal patterns as in previous studies of injury in other tissues . Neutrophils persist after injury for an abnormally long time period in nlrc3-like  mutants, which were shown previously to have excess inflammation and greatly reduced microglia. Our studies also show that normal early vasculature development is not disrupted by macrophage deficiency. The inflammatory conditions in nlrc3-like mutants may impair vasculature re-growth after injury compared with wildtype and with mutants in interferon regulatory factor 8 (irf8), which lack macrophages but, in contrast to nlrc3-like mutants, appear to have a normal inflammatory response. Taken together, these findings point to a possible role of cells of the innate immune system in neurovasculature healing. Our data suggest that immune dysregulation by hyperinflammation or deficiency in macrophage production may have negative consequences on the body’s ability to respond to neurovascular disruption. These studies provide a framework for future studies that may shed light on the pathophysiological process involved in stroke, cancer, and traumatic brain injury.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
